---
title: Cloud Foundry Java Client Library
owner: Java
---

You can use the Cloud Foundry Java Client Library to manage an account on a Cloud Foundry instance.

The Cloud Foundry Java Client Library is available in the [Cloud Foundry Java Client Library](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-java-client) repository on GitHub. The library uses a reactive design based on [Project Reactor](https://projectreactor.io/).


## <a id='adding-the-library'></a> Adding the Java Client Library

To obtain the correct components, see the [Cloud Foundry Java Client Library](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-java-client) repository on GitHub.

Most projects need two dependencies: the Operations API and an implementation of the Client API.
For more information about how to add the Cloud Foundry Java Client Library as dependencies to a Maven or Gradle project, see the sections below.

### <a id='maven'></a> Maven

Add the `cloudfoundry-client-reactor` and `cloudfoundry-operations` dependencies to your `pom.xml` as follows:

```
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.cloudfoundry</groupId>
        <artifactId>cloudfoundry-client-reactor</artifactId>
        <version>VERSION</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.cloudfoundry</groupId>
        <artifactId>cloudfoundry-operations</artifactId>
        <version>VERSION</version>
    </dependency>
    ...
</dependencies>
```

Where `VERSION` is the latest release of the library, for example `5.18.0.RELEASE`. The artifacts are available in Maven Central.

### <a id='gradle'></a> Gradle

Add the `cloudfoundry-client-reactor` and `cloudfoundry-operations` dependencies to your `build.gradle` file as follows:

```
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.cloudfoundry:cloudfoundry-client-reactor:VERSION'
    implementation 'org.cloudfoundry:cloudfoundry-operations:VERSION'
    ...
}
```

Where `VERSION` is the latest release of the library, for example `5.18.0.RELEASE`. The artifacts are available in Maven Central.


## <a id='sample-code'></a> Sample code

The following is a simple sample app that connects to a Cloud Foundry instance, logs in, and displays some information about a space. When running the program, provide the Cloud Foundry API host (for example, `api.example.com`), a valid user name and password, and the org and space as command-line parameters.

```
import org.cloudfoundry.operations.CloudFoundryOperations;
import org.cloudfoundry.operations.DefaultCloudFoundryOperations;
import org.cloudfoundry.reactor.DefaultConnectionContext;
import org.cloudfoundry.reactor.client.ReactorCloudFoundryClient;
import org.cloudfoundry.reactor.tokenprovider.PasswordGrantTokenProvider;

public final class JavaSample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String apiHost = args[0];
        String username = args[1];
        String password = args[2];
        String organization = args[3];
        String space = args[4];

        DefaultConnectionContext connectionContext = DefaultConnectionContext.builder()
            .apiHost(apiHost)
            .build();

        PasswordGrantTokenProvider tokenProvider = PasswordGrantTokenProvider.builder()
            .username(username)
            .password(password)
            .build();

        ReactorCloudFoundryClient cloudFoundryClient = ReactorCloudFoundryClient.builder()
            .connectionContext(connectionContext)
            .tokenProvider(tokenProvider)
            .build();

        CloudFoundryOperations operations = DefaultCloudFoundryOperations.builder()
            .cloudFoundryClient(cloudFoundryClient)
            .organization(organization)
            .space(space)
            .build();

        System.out.printf("%nSpaces:%n");
        operations.spaces().list()
            .doOnNext(s -> System.out.printf("  %s%n", s.getName()))
            .blockLast();

        System.out.printf("%nApplications:%n");
        operations.applications().list()
            .doOnNext(a -> System.out.printf("  %s\t(%s)%n", a.getName(), a.getRequestedState()))
            .blockLast();

        System.out.printf("%nServices:%n");
        operations.services().listInstances()
            .doOnNext(s -> System.out.printf("  %s\t(%s)%n", s.getName(), s.getService()))
            .blockLast();

        connectionContext.dispose();
    }

}
```

For more details about the Cloud Foundry Java Client Library, see the
[Cloud Foundry Java Client Library](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-java-client) repository on GitHub.

To view the operations and objects that you can use, see the [cloudfoundry-operations Javadoc](https://javadoc.io/doc/org.cloudfoundry/cloudfoundry-operations).
